WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.080
Tesla Short's Time Daily, episode 434.

00:00:03.740 --> 00:00:04.720
Hey, good to have you here.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:05.880
It's Patrick and Vancouver.

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:09.260
And this is Tesla Short's Time Daily for April 12th, 2026.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:10.860
Let's get into it.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:12.500
Tesla's full-self driving software

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.180
just cleared its first major European regulatory hurdle.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:18.700
Dutch authorities have officially approved supervised FSD

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:22.160
after more than a year of careful testing and data collection.

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.560
That makes the Netherlands the first country in Europe

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:27.460
to greenlight it, which feels like a genuine breakthrough

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:30.620
after all the back and forth we've seen with European regulators.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:33.560
What stands out to me is that they signed off

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.540
on Tesla's pure vision-only system.

00:00:36.220 --> 00:00:38.720
European safety standards are famously strict

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.260
and the fact that cameras plus neural nets

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:42.500
could meet those requirements,

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:45.060
even with a version that's tuned a bit differently

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:46.640
than what's running in North America,

00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:50.400
is a solid validation of the approach Elon and the team

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.480
have been doubling down on for years.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:54.700
For Dutch customers, this could mean

00:00:54.700 --> 00:00:57.460
supervised full-self driving shows up on public roads

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.180
in the coming months, which is pretty exciting if you live there.

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:02.880
Of course, the broader European Union picture

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.840
is still complicated.

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:05.900
Every other country will need its own review

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:08.080
and that process could drag on for a while

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:11.160
depending on how willing individual governments are to move.

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:14.860
Still, getting that first crack in the regulatory wall matters.

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.860
It proves the system can satisfy regulators

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.180
on this side of the Atlantic

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.620
and that should give the whole autonomy effort

00:01:21.620 --> 00:01:22.660
a bit more momentum.

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:26.260
While that regulatory door is finally opening in Europe,

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.460
Tesla is also quietly rethinking

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:30.700
the more affordable end of its vehicle lineup.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:34.420
Reports confirm the company has restarted serious development

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:36.260
on a lower-priced electric vehicle,

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:38.860
and this time it's shaping up as a compact SUV

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:40.140
rather than a hatchback.

00:01:40.520 --> 00:01:43.360
This revives plans that were essentially put on ice

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.300
last year when Tesla shifted its engineering resources

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.980
toward the Robotaxi and Optimus projects.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.140
It's the kind of strategic pivot we've seen from them before.

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.400
Double down on the future stuff,

00:01:54.500 --> 00:01:56.640
then circle back when market conditions shift.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.040
The timing makes sense.

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.580
Competition in the affordable EV space is heating up fast

00:02:02.180 --> 00:02:04.400
with several manufacturers rolling out smaller,

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:06.140
cheaper battery electric offerings

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:07.180
that are starting to eat

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:09.420
into what used to be Tesla's clear territory.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.240
From a pure business standpoint,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.160
bringing a compact SUV to market could open the door

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:15.920
to a much wider customer base.

00:02:16.120 --> 00:02:19.040
People who want an EV but don't want to pay premium prices,

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.800
that kind of volume growth would help Tesla spread

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:25.220
its fixed manufacturing costs, improve overall margins,

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:27.400
and give the company more breathing room

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.700
as it keeps investing in autonomy and energy.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:32.060
It also shows a refreshing willingness to adapt.

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.000
Tesla isn't married to a particular product roadmap

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:37.000
if the real world tells them something different.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:39.720
That flexibility has served them well in the past,

00:02:40.140 --> 00:02:41.900
and it looks like it's showing up again here.

00:02:42.280 --> 00:02:44.100
Speaking of scaling up in new directions,

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:48.240
Tesla just took a very concrete step on the commercial truck side.

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.560
The company has revealed a dedicated factory in Nevada

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:53.680
that's been purpose-built from the ground up

00:02:53.680 --> 00:02:54.600
to produce the semi.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:57.920
This isn't just adding another line inside an existing building.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:00.600
It's a full standalone facility focused entirely

00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:03.440
on ramping the long-promised class A electric truck.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.040
For years, the semi has been one of those

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:07.260
when it's ready products,

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:10.140
and this new plant feels like Tesla is finally putting

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:13.000
real capital and floor space behind the promise.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.460
From an industry perspective, this is a meaningful signal.

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:19.500
Heavy-duty trucking has always been one of the tougher segments

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:22.220
to electrify because of the range, payload,

00:03:22.520 --> 00:03:23.740
and duty cycle requirements.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:26.420
But if Tesla can actually scale production

00:03:26.940 --> 00:03:29.560
and deliver on the efficiency numbers they've talked about,

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:33.060
logistics operators could see some serious long-term savings

00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:34.260
on fuel and maintenance.

00:03:34.820 --> 00:03:36.860
It could also light a fire under competitors

00:03:36.860 --> 00:03:38.640
who have been moving more cautiously

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:40.260
on battery electric big rigs.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.140
We've seen a few announcements, but nothing that matches

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:45.380
the scale Tesla seems to be preparing for.

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.100
From the heavy-end of the vehicle spectrum,

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.000
we swing all the way to the complete opposite.

00:03:50.460 --> 00:03:52.380
The tiny purpose-built robot taxi.

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:54.820
A fleet of cybercabs has now been spotted

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:56.280
moving around Giga, Texas,

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.640
and the consensus from those who've seen them

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.580
is that volume production ramp might actually be underway.

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:04.300
These aren't prototypes or hand-built show cars anymore.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:06.940
They look like finished vehicles rolling out of the factory,

00:04:06.940 --> 00:04:09.500
which moves the whole robot taxi conversation

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:12.480
from beautiful renderings and keynote slides

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.140
into something you can actually see

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:15.640
and touch on the production floor.

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.800
For Tesla's business model,

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:19.280
this is potentially huge.

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:22.180
If unsupervised autonomy works at scale,

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:24.920
the cybercab could open up an entirely new

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.360
high-margin revenue stream

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.140
that isn't tied to traditional vehicle sales.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.460
We're talking about a future

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:33.400
where the company owns a large chunk of a robot taxi network

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:36.220
instead of just selling the hardware once and moving on.

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:39.360
That would be a fundamental shift in how Tesla makes money

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.000
and the pace at which they can ramp these things

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:43.100
over the next few quarters

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:46.300
is going to be one of the most important metrics to watch.

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:49.720
While the robot taxi future is starting to feel more real,

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:52.620
US regulators just gave one of Tesla's

00:04:52.620 --> 00:04:55.600
existing convenience features a clean bill of health.

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.940
The National Highway Traffic Safety Administration

00:04:57.940 --> 00:04:59.820
has officially closed its investigation

00:04:59.820 --> 00:05:01.740
into the remote parking assist feature

00:05:01.740 --> 00:05:04.080
with no recall or further action required.

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:06.660
That quietly removes a regulatory shadow

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:08.700
that had been hanging over the product for months.

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.880
For owners, it's reassuring to know

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.200
the feature isn't going anywhere

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.720
and will likely keep getting better

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:15.000
through over-the-air updates.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.060
It also highlights something we've seen before.

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.780
Tesla's massive real-world driving data set

00:05:20.780 --> 00:05:23.220
sometimes lets investigations wrap up faster

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.120
than they do for traditional automakers

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:27.060
who rely on more limited crash data.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.180
From a customer experience standpoint,

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:31.540
it's nice when the safety agency looks at the evidence

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:34.160
and says, essentially, there's no systemic problem here.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:35.340
It builds confidence.

00:05:36.060 --> 00:05:38.100
Good news on the regulatory front in the US

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:39.860
and on the sales side in Europe.

00:05:40.320 --> 00:05:42.580
Tesla continues to dominate an important chart.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.820
The Model Y was Germany's best-selling electric vehicle

00:05:45.820 --> 00:05:46.660
again last month.

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:48.520
That's Europe's largest EV market.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.220
And it's impressive that Tesla's highest volume product

00:05:52.220 --> 00:05:53.420
is still sitting at the top

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.000
despite all the new competition that's arrived

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.140
and the broader headwinds the EV segment

00:05:58.140 --> 00:05:59.580
has faced in some countries.

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:01.800
It's a reminder that when buyers are making

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:03.380
real purchasing decisions,

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:07.720
things like proven range, access to a reliable charging network

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:10.500
and everyday usability still carry a lot of weight.

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:14.020
The Model Y isn't the flashiest choice anymore,

00:06:14.200 --> 00:06:16.300
but its practical advantages keep winning.

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:18.660
That strength should give current owners

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:20.740
and anyone thinking about buying one in Europe

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.080
a bit more confidence that they're backing the right horse.

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:25.580
Strong sales performance in Europe

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:28.280
stands in pretty stark contrast to what we're seeing

00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:30.740
with inventory levels back in North America.

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:33.540
Tesla is currently sitting on noticeably

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:36.780
higher vehicle inventory than it has carried in recent memory.

00:06:37.060 --> 00:06:38.680
If you follow the right accounts

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:41.480
or drive past some of the larger staging lots,

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:42.800
the build up is hard to miss.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:45.380
This could be a sign that demand has softened

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:48.580
in certain markets or it might be a deliberate decision

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:51.720
to keep more cars on hand so deliveries can happen faster

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:52.900
once orders come in.

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:55.940
Either way, it creates a real operational challenge.

00:06:56.260 --> 00:06:58.680
Production capacity has clearly outrun

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:01.120
immediate sales momentum in some regions

00:07:01.120 --> 00:07:03.920
and the company will need to manage that surplus

00:07:04.480 --> 00:07:06.500
without resorting to heavy discounting

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:07.380
that would hurt margins.

00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:10.820
We've seen Tesla adjust build rates quickly in the past

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:13.420
and software updates that improve the cars

00:07:13.420 --> 00:07:17.100
already sitting on lots can help move inventory organically.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:20.180
Still, how they balance production with demand

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:22.300
over the next couple of quarters will tell us

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.140
a lot about execution in this environment.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:26.300
It's a legitimate near-term pressure

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:29.000
but not an existential threat given how nimble

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:29.780
they've been before.

00:07:30.460 --> 00:07:32.440
That inventory situation is probably

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:34.480
one of the reasons Tesla and SpaceX

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.380
are both pushing even harder on vertical integration

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:38.920
across their supply chains.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.620
Both companies are deepening their control

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:44.160
over critical components and raw materials

00:07:44.160 --> 00:07:47.500
instead of relying as heavily on outside suppliers.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:51.200
The goal is pretty straightforward, better cost control,

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.340
more consistent quality and the ability to innovate

00:07:54.340 --> 00:07:56.860
faster without waiting for someone else's roadmap.

00:07:57.680 --> 00:07:59.940
For Tesla, this strategy touches everything

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.820
from the vehicles to the energy business

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:04.580
to the specialized hardware needed for AI training

00:08:04.580 --> 00:08:05.320
and inference.

00:08:05.660 --> 00:08:08.200
It's a philosophy the company has followed for a long time.

00:08:08.440 --> 00:08:10.800
Own the pieces that matter most rather than hoping

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:13.560
the broader industry will deliver exactly what you need

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:14.160
when you need it.

00:08:14.480 --> 00:08:16.300
That same mindset is now showing up

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:17.840
in the semiconductor world as well.

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:20.180
There's growing discussion in the community

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:23.720
about closer ties between Tesla, XAI and Intel,

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:26.780
particularly around the possibility of using Intel's

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:28.900
US-based 18A Foundry process

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.900
for next generation AI chips.

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:32.640
If that relationship develops,

00:08:32.920 --> 00:08:35.480
it could give Tesla access to leading-edge compute

00:08:35.480 --> 00:08:38.620
that's both more powerful than what they're constrained to today

00:08:38.620 --> 00:08:40.660
and manufactured domestically.

00:08:41.020 --> 00:08:44.220
That would make scaling Dojo the in-car AI computers

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:46.620
and the hardware for Optimus a lot smoother

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:49.500
without being completely dependent on overseas suppliers.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:53.260
It also has implications for national supply chain resilience

00:08:53.260 --> 00:08:56.300
in a technology area that's becoming strategically important.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:58.940
Vertical integration at the silicon level

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:01.460
would be a natural extension of how Tesla

00:09:01.460 --> 00:09:04.260
already thinks about batteries and power electronics.

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:07.740
Even while all these big bets on AI, autonomy,

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:09.340
and robotics are moving forward,

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:12.280
Tesla hasn't completely taken its eye off the cars

00:09:12.280 --> 00:09:13.540
that still pay the bills today.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:16.040
They've just added a new zen-gray interior option

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:16.900
for the model Y.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:18.380
It's a relatively small change,

00:09:18.380 --> 00:09:20.320
but it gives buyers another aesthetic choice

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:22.140
on the company's highest volume product.

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:23.980
To me, this is a healthy sign.

00:09:24.140 --> 00:09:26.860
Even as the company pours resources into robotaxies

00:09:26.860 --> 00:09:27.780
and humanoid robots,

00:09:28.340 --> 00:09:29.780
it's still polishing the vehicles

00:09:29.780 --> 00:09:31.880
that generate the majority of current revenue

00:09:31.880 --> 00:09:33.880
and keep customers happy in the short term.

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:36.140
For someone ordering a model Y,

00:09:36.460 --> 00:09:38.580
it's a nice bit of extra personalization

00:09:38.580 --> 00:09:40.400
that doesn't change performance or pricing.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:43.920
It shows Tesla can still walk and chew gum at the same time,

00:09:44.320 --> 00:09:45.680
pursuing moonshot initiatives

00:09:45.680 --> 00:09:47.340
while making incremental improvements

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.140
to the cars that are on the road right now.

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:52.020
On the analyst side, there's continued confidence

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.860
and Tesla's vision only approach for robotaxies

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:57.600
despite the near-term pressure on vehicle sales.

00:09:57.880 --> 00:10:00.180
The argument is that a camera-based system

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.200
once it reaches the necessary reliability

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.360
should ultimately be cheaper to scale

00:10:04.360 --> 00:10:06.920
than setups that rely on multiple types of sensors.

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:09.960
That cost advantage could become very important

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:13.420
if robotaxies are going to make economic sense at high volume.

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:15.380
What's interesting is how the conversation

00:10:15.380 --> 00:10:18.060
is becoming more grounded in engineering realities

00:10:18.060 --> 00:10:19.180
instead of pure hype.

00:10:19.680 --> 00:10:22.220
At the same time, it's clear Tesla is shifting

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:25.360
more of its focus and resources toward the higher potential

00:10:25.360 --> 00:10:27.200
businesses of autonomy and robotics

00:10:27.740 --> 00:10:30.460
while traditional car sales face some headwinds.

00:10:30.780 --> 00:10:33.300
That strategic pivot is happening in plain sight.

00:10:33.700 --> 00:10:35.160
We're also hearing fresh reporting

00:10:35.160 --> 00:10:37.920
that the Affordable EV project we talked about earlier

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:39.660
is indeed gaining momentum again

00:10:39.660 --> 00:10:41.640
in the form of that compact SUV.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:44.340
A lot of people had written the idea off

00:10:44.340 --> 00:10:45.720
after the earlier delays.

00:10:46.320 --> 00:10:47.700
So seeing it come back to life,

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.620
suggest Tesla is still committed to expanding its lineup

00:10:50.620 --> 00:10:54.420
downward once it can free up the necessary production capacity.

00:10:54.820 --> 00:10:56.760
On the charging infrastructure side,

00:10:56.920 --> 00:10:59.180
Tesla's new online configurator for commercial

00:10:59.180 --> 00:11:01.980
supercharger deployments is giving fleet operators

00:11:01.980 --> 00:11:03.720
and site owners a clearer picture.

00:11:04.340 --> 00:11:06.620
The all-in price for a meaningful installation

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.620
sits around $940,000.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:10.840
That kind of transparency is helpful,

00:11:11.200 --> 00:11:13.720
but it also drives home just how expensive it still is

00:11:13.720 --> 00:11:15.560
to roll out fast charging at real scale.

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:17.460
The conversation now is shifting

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.020
toward what utilization rates,

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:21.020
business models, or potential partnerships

00:11:21.020 --> 00:11:24.140
are needed to make those numbers work economically over time.

00:11:25.040 --> 00:11:27.580
Interestingly, even though Tesla basically invented

00:11:27.580 --> 00:11:29.900
the modern over-the-air update experience,

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:32.440
BYD has reportedly taken the lead

00:11:32.440 --> 00:11:34.020
in how frequently and effectively

00:11:34.020 --> 00:11:35.880
their vehicles receive new features.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:38.260
That reversal is worth paying attention to.

00:11:38.700 --> 00:11:41.080
It raises real questions about execution speed

00:11:41.080 --> 00:11:42.940
and iteration cadence inside Tesla.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:44.980
For a long time, it felt like a given

00:11:44.980 --> 00:11:46.700
that Tesla would always dominate

00:11:46.700 --> 00:11:48.540
the software-defined vehicle space.

00:11:49.140 --> 00:11:51.380
But competition has a way of sharpening everyone.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:54.380
In the end, customers are the ones who benefit

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:57.960
when multiple companies push hard on frequent useful updates.

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:01.320
Before we wrap up, one thing I'm watching closely

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:04.680
is exactly how that record inventory situation plays out,

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:07.340
sitting on more vehicles than usual can tie up capital

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.020
and put pressure on pricing if it isn't managed carefully,

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.320
especially with softer demand in some markets

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:13.460
and increased competition.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:15.940
That said, Tesla has shown it can adjust

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:18.500
output quickly and clever software improvements

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.700
can make the cars sitting on lots more attractive

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:22.180
without heavy discounting.

00:12:22.300 --> 00:12:25.260
How management balances build rates with real demand

00:12:25.260 --> 00:12:28.000
over the next few quarters will be a pretty good indicator

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:30.220
of how they're navigating the current environment.

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.840
It's a real short-term challenge, but not an existential one.

00:12:34.380 --> 00:12:36.200
And just as a broader thought while we're here,

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:38.220
it's worth applying first principles thinking

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:41.260
to the battery chemistry debate that keeps coming up.

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:43.940
A lot of people assume the chemistry

00:12:43.940 --> 00:12:46.680
with the highest energy density will automatically win

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:50.240
in every application, yet LFP cells are powering

00:12:50.240 --> 00:12:52.820
a growing share of electric vehicles worldwide,

00:12:53.080 --> 00:12:55.200
even though they give up some range per kilogram.

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:57.840
Their lower cost, longer cycle life,

00:12:58.060 --> 00:13:00.900
and better inherent safety often deliver superior

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:04.100
total ownership economics for many real world use cases.

00:13:04.440 --> 00:13:07.340
The real question isn't which chemistry is objectively best.

00:13:07.660 --> 00:13:10.580
It's which combination of vehicle size, daily duty cycle,

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:13.080
local electricity prices and climate

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:16.960
makes one chemistry deliver meaningfully lower lifetime cost

00:13:16.960 --> 00:13:17.680
than another.

00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:20.300
In warm climates with moderate daily distances,

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:23.220
LFP's advantages in upfront price and durability

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:24.200
frequently win.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:27.060
In larger vehicles or cold weather markets,

00:13:27.340 --> 00:13:29.920
the extra range and faster charging of nickel-based cells

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:31.200
can still make more sense.

00:13:31.740 --> 00:13:34.960
Tesla seems to treat battery chemistry as just one variable

00:13:34.960 --> 00:13:37.660
inside a much larger system level optimization

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:39.840
that also includes vehicle architecture,

00:13:40.360 --> 00:13:43.320
thermal management, charging access and smart software.

00:13:43.620 --> 00:13:46.060
They use different cell formats in different regions

00:13:46.060 --> 00:13:48.280
and different products rather than betting everything

00:13:48.280 --> 00:13:49.180
on a single winner.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:53.060
That pragmatic context-aware approach might end up

00:13:53.060 --> 00:13:54.740
being more profitable in the long run

00:13:54.740 --> 00:13:57.040
than chasing one universal best chemistry.

00:13:57.340 --> 00:13:59.760
It's a good reminder that context usually matters

00:13:59.760 --> 00:14:02.120
more than the conventional wisdom wants to admit.

00:14:02.920 --> 00:14:05.120
All right, before we go, keep an eye on how quickly

00:14:05.120 --> 00:14:07.960
those cyber-cab production numbers actually ramp

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:09.900
and whether any additional European countries

00:14:09.900 --> 00:14:12.720
start signaling movement on full self-driving approvals

00:14:12.720 --> 00:14:13.520
in the coming weeks.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:16.640
Those two threads feel like they could shape the next chapter.

